<DocsLayout
  title="Connect a project — Golem Docs"
  description="Pair the Golem Studio plugin with one Golem project using a short-lived code, and understand what the connection covers and how long it lasts."
  heading="Connect a project"
>
  <p>
    Connecting links the Golem panel in <em>this</em> Studio window to <em>one</em> Golem project.
    Until you do it, the plugin is inert: the panel reads <strong>Not connected</strong> and Golem
    cannot see or change anything.
  </p>
  <p>
    The link is made with a short-lived code, so you never copy a token and nothing sensitive ever
    sits on your clipboard.
  </p>

  <h2>Connecting</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Open the place you want Golem to work on in Roblox Studio.</li>
    <li>
      In Studio's <strong>Plugins</strong> tab, click <strong>Golem</strong>. The panel docks and
      shows <strong>Not connected</strong>.
    </li>
    <li>
      In the Golem web workspace, open the project. Until Studio is linked, the conversation ends
      with a <strong>Connect Roblox Studio</strong> block — press <strong>Enter pairing code</strong>
      on its last step. (The <strong>Connect</strong> button in the project's <strong>Studio</strong>
      panel opens the same thing.) You get a six-character code — something like <code>K7M3QP</code>
      — with a countdown.
    </li>
    <li>
      Type that code into the plugin panel and press <strong>Connect</strong>.
    </li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    The panel turns green and reads <strong>Connected</strong> followed by the project name; the
    button becomes <strong>Disconnect</strong>. The web workspace stops saying it is waiting for
    Studio and shows the place you have open. Golem can now act on this place when this project's
    chat asks it to.
  </p>

  <h2>About the code</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>It expires after 10 minutes</strong> and works once. Expired or already used, it is dead — generate a new one.</li>
    <li>
      It is six characters drawn from an alphabet with no confusable letters — no O, I, L or 1 —
      so there is nothing to squint at.
    </li>
    <li>
      Case does not matter and punctuation is ignored, so <code>k7m3qp</code> and
      <code>K7M-3QP</code> both work.
    </li>
    <li>You can have up to five unused codes alive at once, across all your projects.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What the connection covers</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>One Studio window, one project.</strong> Opening a different place, or a second
      Studio window, does not carry the connection with it — connect that window too.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>It survives restarts.</strong> The plugin remembers the session, so reopening Studio
      reconnects without a new code.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>It expires after 30 days.</strong> When it does, the panel says
      <em>Session ended — reconnect with a new code</em>. Generate a fresh code and connect again.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Disconnecting</h2>
  <p>
    Press <strong>Disconnect</strong> in the plugin panel, or disconnect from the web workspace.
    Either one stops Golem acting on the place immediately, whether or not the plugin stays
    installed. Nothing Golem already built is undone — use Studio's undo or a checkpoint for that.
  </p>

  <div class="callout">
    <p>
      <strong>Team Create:</strong> the connection belongs to your Studio window, and Golem's edits
      reach your teammates the same way your own edits do. Teammates who want their own Golem chat
      should connect their own Studio window to their own project.
    </p>
  </div>

  <p>
    Something not lining up? <a href="/docs/troubleshooting">Troubleshooting</a> lists the exact
    messages the panel can show and what each one means.
  </p>
</DocsLayout>
